nodecode-fatetell is a rewrite of RussGuo's RussGuo/fatetell-mcp:

  https://github.com/RussGuo/fatetell-mcp
  commit 5d81776071ddf897abaf3a65ed9f203ca87372a5
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 FateTell (LICENSE)

The original is a package of manifests with no code of its own: it points a
host at FateTell's remote MCP server at https://mcp.fatetell.com/mcp and adds
the fatetell-bazi skill on reading the results. The rewrite reaches the same
server over the same wire -- MCP Streamable HTTP, an initialize handshake and
its initialized notification, then tools/call, with the answer read out of
result.content[] and the "data:" stream framing the endpoint replies with --
and carries the server's tools as tools of its own, with the same argument
names, the same defaults and the same required members the server's own tool
schemas declare: cast_bazi_chart, get_almanac, list_saved_charts and
get_saved_chart (together in fatetell_saved), get_bazi_judgment,
get_daily_fortune, get_chart_calendar and create_saved_chart. The fatetell-bazi skill becomes (help :fatetell),
condensed: the tool choice, the reading rules and the presentation rules are
carried over, its notes and terminology tables are not, because the tool
answers already carry those fields. LICENSE is the repository's LICENSE,
unchanged.

get_fatetell_account is the one server tool with no parameters, and a
contract types at least one member, so it is not a tool here: /fatetell
account asks it for the operator.

What was left out, and why: the host integrations themselves (the Claude,
Cursor and Hermes manifests and the MCP registry entry), because a cell is
loaded by Nodecode and needs no manifest; the OAuth sign-in flow, because the
original let its host run the browser dance and a cell has no flow of its own,
so the token FateTell issues is a section member here and a call that needs it
says so; the interactive chart card (the MCP Apps resource and the skill's
chart-card.html), because Nodecode draws no MCP Apps and the text answer is
the whole chart; and the skill's own prose in full, of which the reading and
presentation rules survive and the tables do not.
